{
  "dom_id": "step-q162990",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{6}{x}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{6}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{3}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}) \\\\  & =(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =10-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
